Description: Let a close tag close back to a matching open ancestor
 A close tag that did not match the current element was dropped, so one
 element left open, such as Drupal's never-closed "<head-placeholder>",
 swallowed every later close tag and nested the rest of the document inside
 it. Following HTML's "any other end tag" step, a close tag that matches an
 open ancestor now leaves the elements in between unclosed and closes the
 ancestor. A close tag that matches nothing open is still dropped. The
 ancestor is found with one walk up the open elements, each element's name
 key is computed once, and every element in between is closed in the same
 step, so closing back over thousands of open elements stays linear.
 An element left unclosed this way, or open at the end of the input, used to
 keep the range of its open tag while its children ran past it, so ESLint's
 getNodeByRangeIndex skipped the whole subtree and getText returned only the
 open tag. Such an element now ends where its last child ends; one with no
 children still ends at its open tag.
Author: Numiko
Origin: vendor
Forwarded: no
Test: test/parser/parse-for-eslint.test.ts ("Twig as part of the markup structure", "parses 20,000 elements left open inside each other, closed back by one close tag", "drops 20,000 stray close tags inside deep nesting", "ends elements that an ancestor's close tag closes where their content ends", "ends elements left open at the end of the file where their content ends"); the "child inside parent" output check covers unclosed elements; upstream snapshot "Custom tag raw content" updated: </markdown> now closes <markdown> and the element left open inside it ends with its text
Last-Update: 2026-10-19
